# Next.js Application Rules

This project uses the Next.js App Router with React Server Components.

## Structure

- All routes live under `src/app/`. Do not create a `pages/` directory.
- Shared UI goes in `src/components/`, grouped into subdirectories by feature.
- Logic shared across routes goes in `src/lib/`, with domain logic under `src/lib/services/`.
- Import with the `@/` alias, for example `@/components/Button`, rather than relative
  paths that climb directories.

## Server and client components

- Components are Server Components by default. Add `'use client'` only when the component
  needs state, effects, or browser APIs.
- Push `'use client'` as far down the tree as possible so that data fetching stays on the server.
- Never import server-only code such as database clients into a client component.

## Data flow

- Use Server Actions for form submissions and mutations. Reach for a route handler in
  `src/app/api/` only when you need an HTTP endpoint for an external consumer.
- Validate all Server Action and route handler input with a Zod schema before use.
  Treat every input as untrusted.
- Prefer the URL for state that should be shareable or survive a reload.

## Notes

- This project has no client state management library installed. If one becomes necessary,
  raise it with the user before adding it.
